/* data/learn-c/phy-11-01.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-11-01.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-11-01"] = {"2.1":{"s":"Physics uses two kinds of quantities. A scalar has only magnitude, such as mass, time or temperature. A vector has both magnitude and direction, such as displacement, velocity, force and acceleration. This chapter explains how to add, subtract and multiply vectors, how to break one into components, and ends with a short introduction to calculus, which is needed in later chapters.","x":["Physical quantities are of two kinds. A scalar has only a magnitude (a number and a unit): mass, time, distance, speed, temperature, energy. A vector has a magnitude and a direction: displacement, velocity, acceleration, force, momentum. Two vectors are equal only if both their magnitudes and directions are equal.","This chapter gives you the tools that you will use in all of physics. First, how to add and subtract vectors. Second, how to split a vector into parts along the axes, which is called resolution. Third, the two ways to multiply vectors, the dot product and the cross product. At the end there is a short course in calculus, with the derivative and the integral, which you need for velocity, acceleration, work and many other topics.","Study tip: do not skip this chapter. A student who is quick with vectors and derivatives solves the later chapters, like motion in a plane and work and energy, much more easily.","Common mistake: adding vectors like numbers. Two forces of 3 N and 4 N do not always give 7 N. The result depends on the angle between them."],"e":"A person walks 3 m east and then 4 m north. The distance covered is 3 + 4 = 7 m, which is a scalar. The displacement is 5 m, at an angle of 53° north of east, which is a vector."},"2.2":{"s":"Vectors are written with an arrow, and drawn as arrows whose length shows the magnitude. Some types: a zero vector has zero magnitude; the negative of a vector has the same size and opposite direction; equal vectors have the same magnitude and direction; a position vector gives the position of a point from the origin. A unit vector has magnitude one, and i, j and k are the unit vectors along x, y and z.","f":[{"x":"u = M/|M|","n":"Unit vector along a vector M: the vector divided by its magnitude.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 2.2, p.17"}],"w":["A unit vector has no unit: it only gives direction."],"x":["A vector is written with an arrow above the letter, or in bold. It is drawn as an arrow. The length of the arrow shows the magnitude, and the arrow head shows the direction. The magnitude of a vector A is written |A| or A.","Some special vectors: a zero (null) vector has zero magnitude and no definite direction. The negative of a vector has the same magnitude and the opposite direction. Equal vectors have the same magnitude and direction, wherever they are drawn. A position vector gives the position of a point as seen from the origin.","A unit vector has a magnitude of exactly 1. It shows a direction only, and it has no unit. The unit vector along a vector M is M/|M|. The unit vectors along the x, y and z axes are i, j and k. So any vector in three dimensions can be written as A = Aₓi + Aᵧj + Azk, and its magnitude is √(Aₓ² + Aᵧ² + Az²).","Common mistake: thinking that a unit vector has the unit of length. It has no unit, because it is a ratio of a vector to its own magnitude."],"e":"For M = 3i + 4j, the magnitude is √(9 + 16) = 5. The unit vector is M/5 = 0.6i + 0.8j. Its magnitude is √(0.36 + 0.64) = 1."},"2.3":{"s":"Vectors are added by the triangle law (place the tail of the second at the head of the first) or by the parallelogram law (draw both from the same point and take the diagonal). Vector addition is commutative and associative. Subtracting a vector means adding its negative. Multiplying a vector by a scalar changes its magnitude (and reverses its direction if the scalar is negative). The size of a sum depends on the angle between the vectors.","f":[{"x":"R = sqrt(P^2 + Q^2 + 2*P*Q*cos(theta))","n":"Magnitude of the resultant of two vectors P and Q with angle theta between them (tails together).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 2.3, p.20"},{"x":"tan(alpha) = Q*sin(theta)/(P + Q*cos(theta))","n":"Direction of the resultant: alpha = angle it makes with P.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 2.3, p.20"}],"w":["The magnitude of a sum is not the sum of magnitudes, unless the vectors point the same way.","For θ = 0° the resultant is P + Q; for θ = 180° it is |P − Q|; for θ = 90° it is √(P² + Q²)."],"x":["Triangle law: to add two vectors, draw the first one, then draw the second with its tail at the head of the first. The sum goes from the tail of the first to the head of the second. Parallelogram law: draw both vectors from the same point, complete the parallelogram, and the diagonal from that point is the sum. Addition of vectors is commutative and associative.","For two vectors P and Q with an angle θ between them, the magnitude of the resultant is R = √(P² + Q² + 2PQ cos θ). The angle α that it makes with P is given by tan α = Q sin θ/(P + Q cos θ). Special cases: for θ = 0°, R = P + Q (the greatest). For θ = 180°, R = |P − Q| (the least). For θ = 90°, R = √(P² + Q²).","To subtract a vector, add its negative: P − Q = P + (−Q). Multiplying a vector by a positive number changes its magnitude but not its direction. Multiplying by a negative number reverses the direction.","Common mistake: using R = P + Q for any angle. The magnitude of a sum is never greater than the sum of the magnitudes, and it is equal only when the vectors point the same way."],"e":"Forces of 10 N and 6 N act at 60° to each other. R = √(100 + 36 + 2 × 10 × 6 × 0.5) = √196 = 14 N. The angle with the 10 N force: tan α = (6 × 0.866)/(10 + 3) = 0.40, so α is about 21.8°."},"2.4":{"s":"A vector can be split into components along chosen directions, usually along perpendicular axes x and y. This is resolution of a vector. For a vector of magnitude R at an angle θ to the x axis, the x component is R cos θ and the y component is R sin θ. Adding or subtracting vectors becomes easy: add the x components together and the y components together. Then rebuild the magnitude and direction.","f":[{"x":"R_x = R*cos(theta), R_y = R*sin(theta)","n":"Rectangular components of a vector R at angle theta to the x axis.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 2.4, p.21"},{"x":"R = sqrt(R_x^2 + R_y^2)","n":"Magnitude from the components.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 2.4, p.21"},{"x":"tan(theta) = R_y/R_x","n":"Direction from the components.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 2.4, p.21"}],"w":["θ is measured from the axis you choose: if you measure from the y axis, sine and cosine swap places."],"x":["Resolution is the opposite of addition: a vector is split into two or more parts, called components, that have the same effect as the vector. Most often, we use perpendicular axes x and y. For a vector R at an angle θ to the x axis: Rₓ = R cos θ and Rᵧ = R sin θ. The magnitude is R = √(Rₓ² + Rᵧ²), and the direction is tan θ = Rᵧ/Rₓ.","How to add many vectors by components: resolve each vector along x and y. Add all the x components to get the total Rₓ, and add all the y components to get Rᵧ. Then find the magnitude and the direction from the two totals. This is the neatest way to add three or more vectors, or to add two vectors at an unusual angle.","A component can be negative. A vector pointing to the left has a negative x component. In a problem on a sloping plane, the best axes are along the plane and perpendicular to it.","Common mistake: using cos for the x component when the angle is measured from the y axis. The cosine goes with the axis from which the angle is measured. If you measure from the y axis, sine and cosine change places."],"e":"A vector of 10 units makes an angle of 30° with the y axis. Along y, the component is 10 cos 30° = 8.66. Along x, it is 10 sin 30° = 5.0. Check: √(8.66² + 5²) = 10."},"2.5":{"s":"There are two ways to multiply two vectors. The scalar (dot) product gives a number: P·Q = PQ cos θ. It is zero for perpendicular vectors, and in components it is PxQx + PyQy + PzQz. The vector (cross) product gives a vector perpendicular to both, with magnitude PQ sin θ and direction from the right hand rule. It is not commutative: P×Q = −(Q×P). Its size is the area of the parallelogram on the two vectors.","f":[{"x":"P.Q = P*Q*cos(theta) = P_x*Q_x + P_y*Q_y + P_z*Q_z","n":"Scalar (dot) product; zero when the vectors are perpendicular, and P.P = P^2.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 2.5, p.23"},{"x":"|P x Q| = P*Q*sin(theta)","n":"Magnitude of the vector (cross) product; zero for parallel vectors.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 2.5, p.24"},{"x":"F = q*(v x B)","n":"Force on a charge q moving with velocity v in a magnetic field B: an example of a cross product.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 2.5, p.24"},{"x":"Area of parallelogram = |P x Q|","n":"Magnitude of the cross product = area of the parallelogram with sides P and Q.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 2.5, p.25"},{"x":"L = r x p","n":"Angular momentum: cross product of the position vector r and the linear momentum p.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 2.5, p.25"}],"w":["i × j = k, j × k = i, k × i = j; reverse the order and the sign changes.","Dot product of perpendicular vectors is 0; cross product of parallel vectors is 0."],"x":["There are two ways to multiply two vectors. The scalar (dot) product gives a number: P·Q = PQ cos θ = PₓQₓ + PᵧQᵧ + PzQz. It is commutative. It is zero for perpendicular vectors, and equal to PQ for parallel vectors. The dot product of a vector with itself is the square of its magnitude. Work, W = F·d, is a dot product, and so is power, P = F·v.","The vector (cross) product gives a vector: P × Q has the magnitude PQ sin θ and its direction is perpendicular to both P and Q, given by the right-hand rule. It is not commutative: P × Q = −(Q × P). It is zero for parallel vectors. Its magnitude is the area of the parallelogram with P and Q as the adjacent sides. Torque τ = r × F, angular momentum L = r × p, and the magnetic force F = q(v × B) are cross products.","Dot products of the axis unit vectors: each one with itself gives 1, and two different ones give 0. For the cross products, i × j = k, j × k = i and k × i = j. If the order is reversed, the sign changes. In components, the cross product is found from a determinant of the unit vectors and the components.","Common mistakes: mixing the two products, and forgetting that the order matters for the cross product. A quick check: if the answer should be a number, it is the dot product."],"e":"A = i + 2j + 3k and B = 2i − j + k. A·B = 2 − 2 + 3 = 3. A × B = (2 + 3)i + (6 − 1)j + (−1 − 4)k = 5i + 5j − 5k, with the magnitude √75 = 8.66."},"2.6":{"s":"Calculus studies how quantities change. Differential calculus finds the rate of change of one quantity with respect to another, as the slope of the tangent to a graph; this is the derivative. Integral calculus does the reverse and finds areas. A few rules help: the derivative of a sum is the sum of the derivatives, plus the product rule, the quotient rule and the chain rule for a function of a function.","f":[{"x":"dy/dx = lim (delta x -> 0) of delta y/delta x","n":"The derivative: the limit of the ratio of the change in y to the change in x; the slope of the tangent.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 2.6, p.26"},{"x":"d(f_1*f_2)/dx = f_1*d(f_2)/dx + f_2*d(f_1)/dx","n":"Product rule.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 2.6, p.26"},{"x":"d/dx f(g(x)) = f'(g(x))*g'(x)","n":"Chain rule for a function of a function.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 2.6, p.26"}],"w":["Do not forget the inner derivative in the chain rule."],"x":["Calculus is the mathematics of change. The derivative dy/dx is the rate of change of y with respect to x. It is the limit of Δy/Δx as Δx tends to zero, and it is the slope of the tangent to the graph. For example, velocity is the derivative of displacement with respect to time, and acceleration is the derivative of velocity.","Rules that you need: d(xⁿ)/dx = n xⁿ⁻¹, and the derivative of a constant is zero. The derivative of a sum is the sum of the derivatives. Product rule: d(f₁f₂)/dx = f₁f₂′ + f₂f₁′. Quotient rule: d(f₁/f₂)/dx = (f₂f₁′ − f₁f₂′)/f₂². Chain rule: d f(g(x))/dx = f′(g(x)) g′(x). Also d(sin x)/dx = cos x, d(cos x)/dx = −sin x, and d(eˣ)/dx = eˣ.","Integration is the reverse of differentiation, and it gives areas. The integral of xⁿ is xⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1) for n not equal to −1. A definite integral from a to b is the area under the graph between a and b: it is the value of the integral at b minus its value at a. For example, the distance moved is the integral of the velocity with time.","Common mistake: forgetting the inner derivative in the chain rule. The derivative of sin 3x is 3 cos 3x, not cos 3x."],"e":"The velocity is v = 3t² m/s. The acceleration is a = dv/dt = 6t, which is 12 m/s² at t = 2 s. The distance moved from t = 0 to t = 2 s is the integral of 3t² dt = t³, which is 8 m."}};
